"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["54909"],{20900(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"captcha-for-login-pages","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/captcha-for-login-pages","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha and the built-in captcha for login and sign-up pages, and use them in Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAPTCHA","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"reCAPTCHA","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/re-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"hCaptcha","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/h-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"Turnstile","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/turnstile"},{"inline":true,"label":"GeeTest","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/gee-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Alibaba Cloud","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/alibaba-cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":7.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"captcha-for-login-pages","title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha and the built-in captcha for login and sign-up pages, and use them in Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CAPTCHA","reCAPTCHA","hCaptcha","Turnstile","GeeTest","Alibaba Cloud","Security"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"}},"content":"For most sign-in and sign-up pages on the global internet, Cloudflare Turnstile is the easiest pick: the free plan has no cap on challenges, and its default mode shows a checkbox only to risky visitors. If many of your users are in mainland China, use Alibaba Cloud Captcha or GeeTest instead, because Cloudflare says Turnstile is not supported there. If you need the captcha to work without any outside service, use the built-in image captcha of Casdoor. Casdoor supports all of these, plus Google reCAPTCHA (v2 and v3) and hCaptcha, as [captcha providers](/docs/provider/captcha/overview), and you pick when the captcha appears per application.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The comparison\\n\\nPricing and availability below were checked against each vendor\'s own site on 2026-11-02. Where a vendor does not publish a number, the table says so instead of guessing.\\n\\n| Provider | What the user sees | Invisible mode | Data and privacy | Free tier and pricing | Mainland China | Casdoor type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor default | Types five digits from an image | No | Generated and checked by your Casdoor server; no third party | Free, part of Casdoor | Works, no outside call | `Default` |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v2 | Checkbox; some users get an image challenge | v2 also has an invisible variant that challenges only suspicious traffic | Google; sets the `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie for risk analysis | Essentials tier free up to 10,000 assessments per month per organization; Premium is $8 flat up to 100,000, then $1 per 1,000 | Google says to use `www.recaptcha.net` where `www.google.com` is not accessible | `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2` (checkbox) |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v3 | Nothing; returns a score | Yes | Same as v2 | Same as v2 | Same as v2 | `reCAPTCHA v3` |\\n| hCaptcha | Checkbox with image challenges on the free plan | Passive mode on Pro (\\"99.9% passive\\") and a no-CAPTCHA mode on Enterprise | Says the free plan complies with GDPR, CCPA, LGPD and PIPL; certifies to the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework | Basic is free; Pro is $139 per month (or $99 per month billed yearly) with 100,000 evaluations, then $0.99 per 1,000; Enterprise through sales | Not documented by hCaptcha | `hCaptcha` |\\n| Cloudflare Turnstile | Managed mode shows a checkbox only when needed | Non-interactive and Invisible widget modes | Cloudflare says it processes only the data strictly necessary and does not read form entries; no Cloudflare proxy required | Free plan: unlimited challenges, up to 20 widgets; Enterprise through sales | Cloudflare: \\"not supported in Mainland China\\" | `Cloudflare Turnstile` |\\n| GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 | One-click, slide puzzle, text click, match-three | Not listed on the v4 product page | Chinese vendor; product-specific privacy policy | No public price list; 30-day free trial, then sales | Chinese vendor | `GEETEST` |\\n| Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 | One-click, slider, jigsaw, image restoration | Traceless mode in the Standard and Enterprise editions | Runs on Alibaba Cloud | Pay as you go: 0.005 CNY per call in mainland China, 0.007 CNY outside; subscription editions and resource packages also available | Yes; Casdoor verifies against the Shanghai region endpoint | `Aliyun Captcha` |\\n\\nTwo notes on the reCAPTCHA rows. Google has moved reCAPTCHA into Google Cloud Fraud Defense with Essentials, Premium and Enterprise tiers, and the free allowance of 10,000 assessments is pooled across the whole Google Cloud organization. Casdoor talks to reCAPTCHA with a site key and secret key through the standard `siteverify` endpoint, and it loads the script from `recaptcha.net` rather than `google.com`, for both the browser script and server-side verification.\\n\\n## Which one to pick\\n\\n### Global SaaS\\n\\nStart with [Cloudflare Turnstile](/docs/provider/captcha/cloudflareTurnstile). The free plan does not meter challenges, the Managed mode keeps most users from clicking anything, and you do not need to route your site through Cloudflare. If you already run on Google Cloud and want reCAPTCHA\'s risk signals, [reCAPTCHA](/docs/provider/captcha/recaptcha) is fine, but watch the 10,000 free assessments: a busy sign-in page or an SMS-pumping attack can pass that in a day. [hCaptcha](/docs/provider/captcha/hcaptcha) is the other common choice; its free plan always shows a challenge, and the passive modes are paid.\\n\\n### An app with users in mainland China\\n\\nTurnstile is out, by Cloudflare\'s own documentation. For reCAPTCHA, Google documents `www.recaptcha.net` as the domain to use when `www.google.com` is not reachable, and Casdoor already uses it, but you should still test from your users\' networks before you depend on it. hCaptcha publishes no statement about China, so the same advice applies.\\n\\nThe two safe picks are [Alibaba Cloud Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/aliyunCaptcha) and [GeeTest](/docs/provider/captcha/geetest). Alibaba Cloud publishes per-call prices and lets you start pay-as-you-go; its traceless mode (in Casdoor, use the `Popup` sub type for it) gets close to the invisible experience of Turnstile. GeeTest sells through its sales team after a 30-day trial. If you serve both China and the rest of the world from one Casdoor, remember that an application has one captcha provider, so split the audiences into two applications if they need different captchas.\\n\\n### Privacy-sensitive users in the EU\\n\\nEvery hosted captcha sends some data about the visitor\'s browser to the vendor. If your legal team wants no third party in the sign-in path at all, the Casdoor [default captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/default) is the only option in the table that keeps everything on your own server. Among the hosted ones, Turnstile and hCaptcha both describe privacy as part of the product (data limited to what the check needs, GDPR and the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework respectively). reCAPTCHA sets a `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie; Google notes that serving from `www.recaptcha.net` avoids other cookies that `www.google.com` may set. Whichever you pick, list it in your privacy notice.\\n\\n### Self-hosted, intranet or offline\\n\\nUse the default captcha. It needs no keys and no outbound network access, so it works in air-gapped installs. Pair it with the `Internet-Only` rule (below) so that people on the office network never see it, while requests from the public internet still do.\\n\\nThe default captcha is the weakest of the group against automated solvers, since it is a plain distorted-digits image. For a public, high-traffic sign-in page, combine it with [multi-factor authentication](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mfa-methods-compared) or move users to [passwordless sign-in](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/passwordless-authentication), which removes the password guessing that captchas exist to slow down.\\n\\n## How CAPTCHA works in Casdoor\\n\\nYou add a provider with **Category** set to `Captcha` and one of these types: `Default`, `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2`, `reCAPTCHA v3`, `hCaptcha`, `Cloudflare Turnstile`, `GEETEST` or `Aliyun Captcha`. Then you add it to an application on the **Providers** tab. An application can hold only one captcha provider; Casdoor refuses to save a second one. Setup steps for each vendor are in the [captcha provider docs](/docs/provider/captcha/overview).\\n\\n### Where Casdoor asks for it\\n\\nThe same provider guards several actions, and Casdoor checks the token on the server, not only in the browser:\\n\\n- Password sign-in.\\n- Sign-up form submission.\\n- Sending a verification code by email or SMS, which covers the code step of sign-up, the forgot-password page and code-based sign-in. This is the protection against bots that burn your SMS budget.\\n\\n### When Casdoor asks for it\\n\\nThe **Rule** of the captcha provider in the application decides when it appears:\\n\\n| Rule         | Behavior                                                               |\\n|---|---|\\n| `None` | Never; the captcha is off |\\n| `Dynamic` | Only after the user\'s failed sign-ins reach the application\'s **Failed signin limit** |\\n| `Always` | Every time |\\n| `Internet-Only` | For requests from public IP addresses; not for private, loopback or other non-public addresses |\\n\\n`Dynamic` is the gentlest setting for sign-in: honest users never see a challenge, and someone guessing passwords hits one after a few failures. It only applies to accounts that already exist, though. A sign-up, or a code sent to an address that has no account yet, does not trigger it. If SMS or email abuse at sign-up is your concern, use `Always` or `Internet-Only`.\\n\\n### Where it appears on the page\\n\\nOn the application\'s sign-in items, the **Captcha** item has its own rule: `Pop up` shows the captcha in a dialog when it is needed, and `Inline` puts it directly on the page. See [Change the captcha](/docs/application/signin-items-table#captcha-rules). Alibaba Cloud Captcha has an extra **Sub type**: `Embed` shows it inside the Casdoor dialog, and `Popup` hands control to Alibaba Cloud\'s own popup, which is what traceless scenes need.\\n\\nUse the **Preview** button on the provider page to check the keys before you turn the rule on.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Google reCAPTCHA versions: https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/versions\\n- Google reCAPTCHA FAQ (`recaptcha.net`, `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie): https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/faq\\n- reCAPTCHA billing in Google Cloud Fraud Defense (checked 2026-11-02): https://docs.cloud.google.com/recaptcha/docs/billing-information\\n- hCaptcha pricing (checked 2026-11-02): https://www.hcaptcha.com/pricing\\n- hCaptcha privacy policy: https://www.hcaptcha.com/privacy\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile overview: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile plans (checked 2026-11-02): https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/plans/\\n- Cloudflare China Network FAQ (Turnstile in mainland China): https://developers.cloudflare.com/china-network/faq/\\n- GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 (checked 2026-11-02): https://www.geetest.com/adaptive-captcha\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 overview: https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/what-is-alibaba-cloud-captcha-2\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 editions and prices (checked 2026-11-02): https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/version-description"},{"id":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: both listen on SMTP port 1025 and web UI port 8025. Compare maintenance, Docker images and test APIs, then point Casdoor at either one.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"MailHog","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/mail-hog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mailpit","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/mailpit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"CI","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/ci"}],"readingTime":8.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: both listen on SMTP port 1025 and web UI port 8025. Compare maintenance, Docker images and test APIs, then point Casdoor at either one.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","MailHog","Mailpit","Testing","SMTP","CI"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/captcha-for-login-pages"},"nextItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"}},"content":"MailHog and Mailpit are both fake SMTP servers for development: they accept every message, keep it, and show it in a web UI instead of delivering it. Both use the same default ports, **SMTP on 1025 and the web UI and API on 8025**, so the SMTP address is `smtp://127.0.0.1:1025` and the inbox is at `http://127.0.0.1:8025` for either one. The difference is maintenance: MailHog\'s last release, v1.0.1, came out in August 2020, while Mailpit released v1.33.0 on October 31, 2026. For a new project, use Mailpit. Casdoor sends to both through a plain SMTP provider, so you can test sign-up codes, password reset emails and magic links without a real mail account.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## MailHog vs Mailpit at a glance\\n\\n| | MailHog | Mailpit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Latest release | v1.0.1, 2020-08-11 | v1.33.0, 2026-10-31 |\\n| Last commit on the default branch | 2022-08-02 | 2026-10-31 |\\n| License | MIT | MIT |\\n| Default SMTP port | 1025 | 1025 |\\n| Default web UI and API port | 8025 | 8025 |\\n| Docker image | `mailhog/mailhog`, amd64 only | `axllent/mailpit`, amd64, arm64 and 386 |\\n| HTTP API | v1 and v2: list, get, delete, search by `from`, `to` or `containing` | v1: list, get, delete, search with filters such as `to:` and `subject:`, `latest` message shortcut |\\n| Default storage | In memory | Temporary SQLite database, deleted on exit |\\n| Persistent storage | MongoDB or Maildir | SQLite file (`MP_DATABASE`) or rqlite |\\n| Message limit | None documented | Keeps the newest 500 by default (`MP_MAX_MESSAGES`) |\\n| HTML check, link check, spam check | No | Yes |\\n| Webhook on new mail | No | Yes (`MP_WEBHOOK_URL`) |\\n| SMTP TLS | Not offered | Optional STARTTLS or SSL/TLS |\\n| Failure injection | Jim | Chaos |\\n| Release a message to a real SMTP server | Yes | Yes |\\n| POP3 server | No | Yes, optional |\\n| Basic auth for the web UI | Yes | Yes |\\n\\nDocker Hub counts show that many people still pull MailHog. That reflects old tutorials and CI files, not activity: an open issue on the MailHog repository titled \\"THIS PROJECT IS DEAD!\\" has a 2023 reply from the author saying he no longer has time to maintain it. Mailpit\'s README describes itself as inspired by MailHog and points to that same comment.\\n\\nThe image architecture matters more than it looks. On an Apple Silicon laptop or an ARM CI runner, `mailhog/mailhog` runs only under emulation, while `axllent/mailpit` has a native arm64 image.\\n\\n## Run either one with Docker\\n\\nMailHog:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailhog -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 mailhog/mailhog\\n```\\n\\nMailpit:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nMailpit keeps mail in a temporary database that disappears when the container stops. To keep it across restarts, mount a volume and set `MP_DATABASE`:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit \\\\\\n  -v \\"$PWD/mailpit-data:/data\\" \\\\\\n  -e MP_DATABASE=/data/mailpit.db \\\\\\n  -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 \\\\\\n  axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nWithout Docker, `brew install mailhog` and `brew install mailpit` both work on macOS, and Mailpit ships static binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS on its releases page.\\n\\nIn GitHub Actions, run it as a service container next to your test job:\\n\\n```yaml\\nservices:\\n  mailpit:\\n    image: axllent/mailpit\\n    ports:\\n      - 1025:1025\\n      - 8025:8025\\n```\\n\\n## Point Casdoor at MailHog or Mailpit\\n\\nCasdoor has no separate provider type for MailHog or Mailpit, and it doesn\'t need one. The `Default` email type sends over SMTP, and both tools are SMTP servers.\\n\\n1. As a global administrator, add a provider in the `admin` or `built-in` organization.\\n1. Defina **Category** como `Email` e **Type** como `Default`.\\n1. Set **Host** to where the tool runs and **Port** to `1025`.\\n1. Leave **Username** and **Password** empty.\\n1. Click **Test SMTP Connection**, then **Send Testing Email**, and check the web UI on port 8025.\\n\\nThe step-by-step pages with screenshots are [Test email with MailHog](/docs/provider/email/mailhog) and [Test email with Mailpit](/docs/provider/email/mailpit).\\n\\nA few details decide whether this works on the first try:\\n\\n**Leave SSL mode on Auto, or set it to Disable.** In `Auto`, Casdoor uses implicit TLS only on port 465 and upgrades with STARTTLS only if the server offers it. Neither MailHog nor a default Mailpit offers STARTTLS on port 1025, so the connection stays plain. Don\'t choose `Enable`: that forces implicit TLS, and the handshake fails against a plain-text server. The [SSL mode table](/docs/provider/email/overview#ssl-mode) has the full behavior.\\n\\n**Leave the credentials empty.** Casdoor logs in to the SMTP server only when **Username** is set. If you fill it in, Go\'s `PLAIN` authentication refuses to send the password over an unencrypted connection unless the host is localhost, so a MailHog at `192.168.x.x` fails with an authentication error even though MailHog would accept any login. Mailpit by default doesn\'t ask for authentication at all.\\n\\n**Use the right host.** `127.0.0.1` works only when Casdoor and the mail catcher run on the same machine. If both run in Docker Compose, use the service name, such as `mailpit`, as the host.\\n\\n**Use a global provider.** A provider owned by an ordinary organization may only reach Internet addresses, so it can\'t connect to `127.0.0.1` or a private IP. Providers in `admin` or `built-in` aren\'t restricted. See [Which addresses a provider can reach](/docs/provider/overview#which-addresses-a-provider-can-reach).\\n\\n**Set `origin` for links.** Verification codes work right away. Password reset links and magic links need Casdoor to know its public URL, through `origin` in `conf/app.conf` or a request on a loopback host. Otherwise the reset link is dropped from the email and magic links fail. See [Templates](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders).\\n\\nFinally, add the provider to your application in [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers) so sign-up and password reset actually use it.\\n\\n## Read the verification code in an automated test\\n\\nCasdoor\'s verification codes are six digits, and the default template reads \\"Here is your code: \\" followed by the code. An end-to-end test signs up a user, asks Casdoor to send the code, then reads it from the mail catcher\'s API.\\n\\nWith Mailpit, search by recipient and open the newest match. `GET /api/v1/search` returns matches newest first, and `GET /api/v1/message/{ID}` returns the parsed `Text` and `HTML` bodies:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { request } from \\"@playwright/test\\";\\n\\nconst MAILPIT = process.env.MAILPIT_URL ?? \\"http://127.0.0.1:8025\\";\\n\\nexport async function latestCode(to: string): Promise<string> {\\n  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: MAILPIT });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\\n    const res = await api.get(\\"/api/v1/search\\", {\\n      params: { query: `to:\\"${to}\\"`, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n    });\\n    const { messages } = await res.json();\\n    if (messages.length > 0) {\\n      const msg = await (await api.get(`/api/v1/message/${messages[0].ID}`)).json();\\n      const match = (msg.Text || msg.HTML).match(/code: (\\\\d{6})/);\\n      if (match) return match[1];\\n    }\\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(`no verification email for ${to}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf only one test sends mail at a time, `GET /api/v1/message/latest` skips the search step. Before each test, `DELETE /api/v1/messages` with no body clears the inbox.\\n\\nWith MailHog, the closest call is `GET /api/v2/search?kind=to&query=<address>`. Messages come back newest first in `items`, and the body is in `items[0].Content.Body`:\\n\\n```ts\\nconst res = await api.get(\\"/api/v2/search\\", {\\n  params: { kind: \\"to\\", query: to, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n});\\nconst { items } = await res.json();\\nconst body: string = items[0]?.Content.Body ?? \\"\\";\\n```\\n\\n`Content.Body` is the raw MIME body. Casdoor sends quoted-printable, so `=` becomes `=3D` and long lines get soft breaks ending in `=`. Decode it first, or keep the code near the start of a short template. Clear MailHog with `DELETE /api/v1/messages`.\\n\\n## Migrating from MailHog to Mailpit\\n\\nBecause the ports match, most migrations are a one-line change in `docker-compose.yml`: replace `mailhog/mailhog` with `axllent/mailpit`. Casdoor\'s provider keeps the same host and port. What does change:\\n\\n- **Settings.** MailHog reads `MH_*` variables; Mailpit reads `MP_*`. If you used `MH_STORAGE=maildir` or MongoDB, move to `MP_DATABASE`.\\n- **Test helpers.** Mailpit has no `/api/v2` endpoints. Rewrite calls to `/api/v2/messages` and `/api/v2/search` for `/api/v1/messages` and `/api/v1/search`, and read `messages[].ID` plus `Text` or `HTML` instead of `items[].Content.Body`. The quoted-printable decoding goes away.\\n- **Search syntax.** `kind=to&query=a@example.com` becomes `query=to:\\"a@example.com\\"`.\\n- **Failure injection.** Jim has no flags in Mailpit; use Chaos.\\n- **Authentication.** If an app insists on logging in over plain SMTP, start Mailpit with `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ACCEPT_ANY=1` and `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE=1`. For Casdoor, leaving the credentials empty is simpler.\\n- **Retention.** Mailpit prunes to the newest 500 messages by default. Raise `MP_MAX_MESSAGES` if a long test run needs more.\\n\\n## Going to production\\n\\nMailHog and Mailpit never deliver mail to real inboxes unless you release messages by hand, so production needs a real provider. In Casdoor you swap the provider, not the templates. The email templates, the `%s` code placeholder and the `<reset-link>` block stay the same:\\n\\n- Any SMTP server with the `Default` type, described in [Email providers](/docs/provider/email/overview)\\n- [SendGrid](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid) and [Resend](/docs/provider/email/resend) through their APIs\\n- [Brevo](/docs/provider/email/brevo) through its SMTP relay\\n- [Azure Communication Services](/docs/provider/email/azureACS)\\n- Your own HTTP endpoint with [Custom HTTP Email](/docs/provider/email/customHttp)\\n\\nA common setup keeps Mailpit in development and CI and the real provider in production. Each environment points its application at a different provider, so the sign-up flow you test is the one that ships.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- MailHog repository and README: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog\\n- MailHog configuration and API v1/v2 docs: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/tree/master/docs\\n- MailHog maintenance comment: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/issues/442#issuecomment-1493415258\\n- Mailpit repository and README: https://github.com/axllent/mailpit\\n- Mailpit documentation (Docker, storage, SMTP, webhook, search filters): https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/\\n- Mailpit API v1 reference: https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/api-v1/\\n- Casdoor email provider code: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/email"},{"id":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","description":"The OAuth callback URLs that Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT use for MCP servers, where claude_desktop_config.json lives, and how to register each client in Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cursor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/cursor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/claude"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChatGPT","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/chat-gpt"},{"inline":true,"label":"Dynamic Client Registration","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/dynamic-client-registration"}],"readingTime":7.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","description":"The OAuth callback URLs that Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT use for MCP servers, where claude_desktop_config.json lives, and how to register each client in Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","MCP","OAuth","Cursor","Claude","ChatGPT","Dynamic Client Registration"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"}},"content":"Every MCP client sends its own redirect URI when it signs in to an OAuth-protected MCP server. Cursor desktop uses `http://localhost:8787/callback` (releases before mid-2026 used `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback`), Cursor on the web and Cursor Cloud Agents use `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback`, claude.ai, Claude Desktop connectors, Claude mobile and Cowork use `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, and ChatGPT uses `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect` or a per-connection `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>`. If you turn on dynamic client registration in Casdoor, you don\'t enter any of these: the clients register themselves or hand Casdoor a client ID metadata document. You add them to **Redirect URLs** only when you create a Casdoor application for a client by hand.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Redirect URIs by client\\n\\n| Client | Redirect URI | How it gets a client ID from Casdoor | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Cursor desktop (current) | `http://localhost:8787/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` in `mcp.json` | Fixed port 8787 |\\n| Cursor web and Cloud Agents | `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` | With DCR, the desktop app registers this URI and the localhost one |\\n| Cursor desktop (older releases) | `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Cursor made the loopback URI the default in July 2026 |\\n| claude.ai, Claude Desktop connectors, Claude mobile, Cowork | `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` | CIMD, then DCR, or a client ID entered in the custom connector dialog | Anthropic\'s servers make the requests |\\n| Claude Code | `http://localhost:<port>/callback` and `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` | CIMD | The port changes per session |\\n| Claude Desktop through `mcp-remote` | `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` | DCR | The port is derived from the server URL |\\n| ChatGPT | `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect`, or `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` | CIMD, DCR, or a predefined client | Copy the exact value from ChatGPT |\\n\\nThese values come from the vendors\' documentation as of November 2, 2026 (see [References](#references)). Clients change them. When a sign-in fails, read the `redirect_uri` parameter of the authorization request in the browser\'s address bar and compare it with what Casdoor has.\\n\\nThe table applies in two setups: the [MCP server built into Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/overview) at `/api/mcp`, and your own MCP server that uses [Casdoor as its authorization server](/docs/mcp-auth/overview). In both, the client finds Casdoor through protected resource metadata ([RFC 9728](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728)) and gets its client ID from Casdoor.\\n\\n## Option 1: let clients register themselves\\n\\nCasdoor supports dynamic client registration ([RFC 7591](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7591)) at `/api/oauth/register`, and client ID metadata documents (CIMD), where the `client_id` is an HTTPS URL of a JSON document that lists the client\'s redirect URIs. One switch controls both: **Enable dynamic client registration** on the organization. It is off by default, for every organization including `built-in`.\\n\\nWith the switch on for `built-in`, the discovery document shows what the clients look for:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://your-casdoor.com/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq \'{\\n  registration_endpoint,\\n  client_id_metadata_document_supported,\\n  code_challenge_methods_supported,\\n  token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported,\\n  authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\n}\'\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"registration_endpoint\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/oauth/register\\",\\n  \\"client_id_metadata_document_supported\\": true,\\n  \\"code_challenge_methods_supported\\": [\\"S256\\"],\\n  \\"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\\": [\\"client_secret_basic\\", \\"client_secret_post\\", \\"private_key_jwt\\", \\"none\\"],\\n  \\"authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEach client then picks its method:\\n\\n- **Claude** uses CIMD only when the metadata has both `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true` and `none` in `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`. Casdoor has both once the switch is on, so Claude identifies itself with a document URL and Casdoor creates an application named `cimd_...`. Otherwise Claude falls back to DCR.\\n- **ChatGPT** prefers CIMD, with the `client_id` `https://chatgpt.com/oauth/client.json` (or a per-callback variant), and uses DCR when CIMD isn\'t available.\\n- **Cursor** registers through DCR as a public client without a secret. Cursor\'s documentation doesn\'t mention CIMD.\\n- **`mcp-remote`** registers through DCR.\\n\\nThe redirect URIs come from the registration request or the metadata document, so there is nothing to type. Registered clients run under a restricted role and their tokens can\'t call the management API. See [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration) for the full rules and the organization a client lands in.\\n\\n## Option 2: create an application for the client\\n\\nIf you keep registration off, create one application per client, so that you can revoke them separately:\\n\\n1. No console de administra\xe7\xe3o do Casdoor, v\xe1 para **Identity** > **Applications** e adicione uma aplica\xe7\xe3o, por exemplo, `cursor-mcp`.\\n1. Add the client\'s redirect URIs from the table to **Redirect URLs**. For Cursor, add all three, because different Cursor installs send different ones.\\n1. Set **Category** to `Agent`. See [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup) for scopes and grant types.\\n1. Give the **Client ID** to the client.\\n\\nA client secret is optional for these clients. Casdoor accepts an authorization code exchange without a secret when the request used PKCE, and Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT all use PKCE with `S256`. If a client does send a secret, it must be the right one.\\n\\nWhere the client ID goes:\\n\\n- **Cursor**: an `auth` object in `~/.cursor/mcp.json` or `.cursor/mcp.json`. With a static `CLIENT_ID`, the Cursor desktop app sends only its localhost redirect URI.\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"url\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\",\\n        \\"auth\\": {\\n          \\"CLIENT_ID\\": \\"${env:CASDOOR_MCP_CLIENT_ID}\\",\\n          \\"scopes\\": [\\"application:read\\", \\"user:read\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Claude**: the OAuth client ID field in the custom connector dialog. Register `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`.\\n- **ChatGPT**: ChatGPT also works with predefined OAuth clients. Copy the redirect URI from the MCP server\'s management page in ChatGPT, since which of the two forms it uses depends on the authorization server.\\n\\nStep-by-step guides: [Cursor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-cursor), [Claude Desktop](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-claude-desktop), [ChatGPT](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-chatgpt).\\n\\n## How Casdoor matches redirect URIs\\n\\nCasdoor compares the `redirect_uri` of a request with each entry in **Redirect URLs**:\\n\\n- The scheme, the host and the path must match. The query string isn\'t compared.\\n- The port must match, except on loopback hosts. When both URIs use the same loopback host (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`), Casdoor ignores the port, as [RFC 8252](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3) asks for native apps. `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are different hosts, so Claude Code needs both `http://localhost/callback` and `http://127.0.0.1/callback`.\\n- A redirect URI on a subdomain of a registered host also matches, so `https://example.com/cb` accepts `https://app.example.com/cb`. Register the most specific host you can.\\n- Custom schemes such as `cursor://` match by the same rules. `javascript:`, `data:` and `vbscript:` URIs are always rejected.\\n\\nBecause the loopback port is ignored, an `mcp-remote` or Claude Code registration keeps working when the local port changes between sessions.\\n\\n## Where claude_desktop_config.json lives\\n\\nThe [MCP documentation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) gives these paths. In Claude Desktop, **Settings** > **Developer** > **Edit Config** opens the file and creates it if it\'s missing.\\n\\n| Operating system | Path                                                              |\\n|---|---|\\n| macOS            | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |\\n| Windows          | `%APPDATA%\\\\Claude\\\\claude_desktop_config.json`                   |\\n| Linux            | `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`                     |\\n\\nClaude Desktop reaches a remote MCP server in one of two ways, and they use different redirect URIs:\\n\\n- **As a connector**, added in Claude\'s connector settings. Anthropic\'s servers run the OAuth flow, the redirect URI is `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, and Casdoor must be reachable from the internet.\\n- **Through `claude_desktop_config.json`**, with `mcp-remote` as a local bridge. The OAuth flow runs on your computer, and the redirect URI is `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback`:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"command\\": \\"npx\\",\\n        \\"args\\": [\\"mcp-remote\\", \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\"]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\nQuit Claude Desktop completely and start it again after you edit the file.\\n\\n## Hosted clients need a public Casdoor\\n\\nChatGPT and Claude connectors call your MCP server and Casdoor from their vendors\' servers, not from your computer. Casdoor must be reachable over public HTTPS with a valid certificate. Anthropic\'s requests come from `160.79.104.0/21`, if you filter by IP. Cursor desktop, Claude Code and `mcp-remote` run the flow locally, so a Casdoor on your network works for them.\\n\\nTwo more things hosted clients check, which Casdoor already does:\\n\\n- **Issuer in the response** ([RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207)): ChatGPT rejects an authorization response without the right `iss`, and uses its stable redirect URI only with authorization servers that meet its RFC 9207 requirements. Casdoor advertises the support in its metadata and adds `iss` to the redirect.\\n- **Resource indicators** ([RFC 8707](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707)): ChatGPT sends `resource` with the MCP server URL on the authorization and token requests. Casdoor puts that value in the `aud` claim of the access token, and your MCP server should check it. See [MCP authentication](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/authentication) for the discovery flow.\\n\\nRelated reading on www.casdoor.com: [OAuth for AI agents](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-for-ai-agents) and [authorization servers for MCP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mcp-authorization-servers).\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Cursor: [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://cursor.com/docs/mcp), section \\"Static OAuth for remote servers\\"\\n- Cursor forum: [OAuth redirect URI changed from cursor:// to http://localhost](https://forum.cursor.com/t/oauth-redirect-uri-changed-from-cursor-to-http-localhost-for-streamable-http-mcp/165019)\\n- Anthropic: [Authentication for connectors](https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication)\\n- Model Context Protocol: [Connect to local MCP servers](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers)\\n- OpenAI: [Authenticate users](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/auth)\\n- `mcp-remote`: [README](https://github.com/geelen/mcp-remote)\\n- Casdoor: [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration), [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup)"},{"id":"nginx-sso-authentication","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/nginx-sso-authentication","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"Add CAS, OIDC or auth_request login in front of any app behind nginx with Casdoor: forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, an nginx module, CAS clients and MFA compared.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"nginx","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/nginx"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAS","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/cas"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"forward auth","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/forward-auth"}],"readingTime":10.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"nginx-sso-authentication","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"Add CAS, OIDC or auth_request login in front of any app behind nginx with Casdoor: forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, an nginx module, CAS clients and MFA compared.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","nginx","CAS","SSO","OIDC","MFA","forward auth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"},"nextItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"}},"content":"Open-source nginx has no CAS module, so \\"nginx CAS authentication\\" in practice means one of two setups. Either nginx asks an authentication service about every request through its `auth_request` module, and Casdoor answers (directly at `/api/forward-auth`, or through casdoor-forward-auth or OAuth2 Proxy), or the app behind nginx is itself a CAS client and talks to Casdoor\'s CAS server endpoints. If you can rebuild nginx, the Casdoor nginx module runs the login inside nginx, and if you\'d rather not run nginx at all, Casdoor\'s site proxy can sit in front of the app. In every case the login page is Casdoor\'s, so one SSO session, one set of permissions and one MFA policy cover all the apps.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The options at a glance\\n\\n| Option | Login logic runs in | What nginx needs | Protocol to Casdoor | Who gets in | Pick it when |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor forward auth | Casdoor, `/api/forward-auth` | `auth_request` | Casdoor session cookie on the app\'s domain | Application permissions, checked again at least once a minute | nginx is already in front and you want no extra service |\\n| casdoor-forward-auth | A small Go service | `auth_request` | OIDC authorization code | Casdoor roles or groups, per location | One session cookie shared by many subdomains |\\n| OAuth2 Proxy | OAuth2 Proxy | `auth_request` | OIDC | `allowed_groups`, email domains | You already run OAuth2 Proxy |\\n| casdoor-nginx-module | nginx itself | A compiled module | OIDC with PKCE, tokens verified locally | Any signed-in user of the app | No extra process, plus bearer tokens for APIs |\\n| CAS client in the app | The app | Nothing, it only proxies | CAS 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 | Whatever the app does with the attributes | The app already supports CAS |\\n| Casdoor site proxy | Casdoor on ports 80 and 443 | No nginx | Casdoor session | Application permissions | Nothing else is in front of the app |\\n\\nThe first three differ only in who answers nginx\'s subrequest.\\n\\n## Why there is no \\"nginx CAS module\\"\\n\\nCAS is a conversation between an application and the CAS server: the app sends the browser to the server\'s `/login` with a `service` URL, gets a service ticket back on that URL, and validates the ticket with a server-to-server call. Apache httpd has Apereo\'s [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas) for doing this at the web server, but nginx has no CAS module among its official modules. For nginx, the standard building block is [`ngx_http_auth_request_module`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html): before serving a request, nginx sends a subrequest to a URL you choose. A 2xx answer lets the request through, 401 or 403 denies it with that code, and any other code is an error.\\n\\nTo the user, a CAS login and an OIDC login look the same: a redirect to the Casdoor login page and back. So for an app without CAS support, the practical answer is OIDC at the nginx layer. Background on the protocol: [What is CAS](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-cas).\\n\\nThe auth_request module is not built by default: add `--with-http_auth_request_module` when compiling, or check `nginx -V` on a packaged nginx.\\n\\n## Option 1: Casdoor\'s own forward auth\\n\\nCasdoor can answer nginx\'s subrequest itself. You add a **Site** in Casdoor whose **Domain** is the app\'s domain and whose **Casdoor app** is the application users sign in to, and add `https://app.example.com/caswaf-handler` to that application\'s **Redirect URLs**. Host and port can stay empty, since the traffic doesn\'t pass through Casdoor.\\n\\nBecause `auth_request` can\'t follow a redirect, Casdoor answers an unauthenticated subrequest with `401` and puts the login URL in `Location`. nginx turns that into a 302:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    proxy_buffer_size 16k;\\n    proxy_buffers 8 16k;\\n\\n    location = /caswaf-handler {\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Uri $request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location = /casdoor-auth {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n        proxy_set_header X-Original-URL $scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Method $request_method;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /casdoor-auth;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_redirect $upstream_http_location;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_email $upstream_http_x_forwarded_email;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_groups $upstream_http_x_forwarded_groups;\\n        error_page 401 =302 $casdoor_redirect;\\n\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Email $casdoor_email;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Groups $casdoor_groups;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:3000;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe backend receives the user in `X-Forwarded-User`, `X-Forwarded-Email` and `X-Forwarded-Groups`. Access follows a [permission](/docs/permission/permission-configuration) on the application, checked again at least once a minute, so a disabled user loses access quickly. The site\'s **Public paths** (webhooks, health checks) pass without a login.\\n\\nSee [Using your own reverse proxy](/docs/site/overview#using-your-own-reverse-proxy-forward-auth) for the reference and [Add login to an app without OIDC](/docs/site/protect-app) for a Grafana walkthrough. The app must only be reachable through nginx, or anyone can send `X-Forwarded-User` themselves.\\n\\n## Option 2: casdoor-forward-auth or OAuth2 Proxy\\n\\nIf you would rather not create a Casdoor site for each app, run a small service that does the OIDC flow against Casdoor and answers the subrequest.\\n\\n[casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth) is Casdoor\'s standalone service for this (it replaced traefik-casdoor-auth). It keeps the user in a signed cookie with no server state. For nginx it exposes `/verify`, which answers 200 with identity headers or 401, and nginx redirects to its `/login` on 401:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    location = /_casdoor_verify {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor-forward-auth:9999/verify?groups=built-in/dev;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    location @casdoor_login {\\n        return 302 https://auth.example.com/login?rd=$scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /_casdoor_verify;\\n        error_page 401 = @casdoor_login;\\n\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_roles $upstream_http_x_forwarded_roles;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Roles $casdoor_roles;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:8080;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe `roles` and `groups` query parameters on the verify URL restrict each app to holders of a Casdoor role or members of a group; signed-in users without them get 403. Roles and groups are read at login and kept for the session, so changes apply after the user signs in again. Setting `COOKIE_DOMAIN=example.com` lets one login cover every subdomain. The [Traefik guide](/docs/integration/go/traefik) covers the same service for Traefik.\\n\\n[OAuth2 Proxy](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx) works the same way with `provider = \\"oidc\\"` and `oidc_issuer_url` set to your Casdoor URL. Its `/oauth2/auth` endpoint returns 202 or 401, user and email headers need `--set-xauthrequest`, and the forwarded-header handling in its nginx example requires `--reverse-proxy`. Two Casdoor settings matter: use the `JWT-Standard` token format, or users with an unverified email are rejected, and keep the `profile` scope so groups and roles are returned. See [OAuth2 Proxy with Casdoor](/docs/integration/go/oauth2-proxy) and the [nginx community edition guide](/docs/integration/C++/NginxCommunityVersion).\\n\\n## Option 3: the Casdoor nginx module\\n\\n[casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module) does the login inside nginx, without a separate process. Browsers without a session go to Casdoor\'s login page; the module exchanges the code with PKCE, verifies the access token\'s signature, `exp`, `aud` and type against the Casdoor certificate, and keeps the user in a signed cookie. API clients can send `Authorization: Bearer` with a Casdoor access token, verified locally without a call to Casdoor.\\n\\nIt needs nginx 1.22 or newer and OpenSSL 1.1.1 or newer. You can build it as a dynamic module against the nginx you already run:\\n\\n```bash\\n./configure --with-compat --with-http_ssl_module --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/casdoor-nginx-module\\nmake modules\\ncp objs/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so /etc/nginx/modules/\\n```\\n\\n```nginx\\nload_module modules/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so;\\n\\nevents {}\\n\\nhttp {\\n    server {\\n        listen 443 ssl;\\n        server_name app.example.com;\\n        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/app.crt;\\n        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/app.key;\\n\\n        casdoor_endpoint        https://door.example.com;\\n        casdoor_client_id       <client-id>;\\n        casdoor_client_secret   <client-secret>;\\n        casdoor_certificate     /etc/nginx/casdoor.pem;\\n        casdoor_redirect_uri    https://app.example.com/casdoor/callback;\\n        casdoor_token_location  /_casdoor/token;\\n\\n        location = /_casdoor/token {\\n            internal;\\n            proxy_pass https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token;\\n            proxy_ssl_server_name on;\\n        }\\n\\n        location = /casdoor/callback {\\n            casdoor_callback;\\n        }\\n\\n        location / {\\n            casdoor on;\\n            proxy_set_header X-User $casdoor_user;\\n            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nReplace the placeholders with the application\'s values. The session isn\'t checked with Casdoor again until it expires, so disabling a user takes effect when the session ends; `casdoor_session_timeout` shortens that window. The module has no role or group checks. On NGINX Plus, see [the NGINX Plus guide](/docs/integration/C++/Nginx) instead.\\n\\n## Option 4: the app is a CAS client\\n\\nIf the app already speaks CAS, such as a Java app with the Apereo CAS client, nginx only proxies and Casdoor is the CAS server. Each Casdoor application has CAS endpoints under `/cas/<organization>/<application>`: `/login` and `/logout`, `/validate` for CAS 1.0, `/serviceValidate`, `/proxyValidate` and `/proxy` for 2.0, `/p3/serviceValidate` and `/p3/proxyValidate` for 3.0, and `/samlValidate` for SAML 1.1 validation.\\n\\nThe app sends the browser to `/login?service=...`, Casdoor redirects back with `ticket=ST-...`, and the app validates it:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl \\"https://door.example.com/cas/my-org/my-app/p3/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234567890&service=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2F\\"\\n```\\n\\nA successful answer is a `cas:serviceResponse` with `cas:user` and attributes such as email, display name and `memberOf`; add `format=json` for JSON. Things that trip people up:\\n\\n- Every `service` URL must be listed in the application\'s **Redirect URLs**, and the `service` at validation must exactly match the one the ticket was issued for.\\n- Tickets work once and expire after 5 minutes.\\n- Tickets are kept in memory, so with several Casdoor replicas, validation must reach the replica that issued the ticket (sticky sessions).\\n- Behind nginx, pass `Host` and `X-Forwarded-Proto` so the app builds the same `service` URL that the browser saw.\\n\\nThe setup, including the Java client\'s filters for each CAS version, is in [Connect a CAS client](/docs/how-to-connect/cas).\\n\\n## Option 5: let Casdoor be the reverse proxy\\n\\nWhen nothing else sits in front of the app, Casdoor can serve it directly: its site proxy listens on `gatewayHttpPort` and `gatewayHttpsPort` (80 and 443 by default) once a site exists. Set the site\'s **Host** to the backend, pick a certificate, set **Casdoor app**, and point DNS at Casdoor. Login, permissions and headers behave as in option 1. See [Sites](/docs/site/overview).\\n\\n## Adding MFA\\n\\nMFA needs no change in nginx or in the app. Casdoor asks for the second factor on its own sign-in page before it issues an OAuth authorization code or a CAS service ticket, so every option above gets it. Users can use a TOTP app, SMS, email or RADIUS, with recovery codes, and administrators can require MFA for an organization with [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items). That also answers \\"CAS 2FA\\": with Casdoor as the CAS server, the second factor is part of the CAS login.\\n\\nMFA is enforced at login, not per request: a session that nginx or a forward-auth service already holds lasts until it expires, and the organization\'s **MFA remember time** lets users skip the second factor on a trusted device for a while. See the [MFA guide](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication) and [What is MFA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-mfa).\\n\\nOnce signed in to Casdoor, users reach other apps that send them to Casdoor without a new password prompt; with **Auto signin**, Casdoor also skips the account picker. See [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) and [Single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout).\\n\\n## Which one to pick\\n\\n- No login in the app and nginx already in front: Casdoor\'s forward auth.\\n- One login across many subdomains, or role checks per location: casdoor-forward-auth (or OAuth2 Proxy if you already run it).\\n- You build nginx yourself and also protect APIs with bearer tokens: casdoor-nginx-module.\\n- The app supports CAS: Casdoor\'s CAS endpoints, nginx as a plain proxy.\\n- No proxy yet: Casdoor\'s site proxy.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- nginx: [ngx_http_auth_request_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html)\\n- OAuth2 Proxy: [nginx integration](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx)\\n- Apereo: [CAS Protocol Specification](https://apereo.github.io/cas/7.1.x/protocol/CAS-Protocol-Specification.html), [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas)\\n- Casdoor: [casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth), [casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Sites](/docs/site/overview), [Connect a CAS client](/docs/how-to-connect/cas), [MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication)"},{"id":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","description":"Add single sign-on to Spring Boot with OpenID Connect and Casdoor: oauth2Login web apps, JWT resource servers and Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, with YAML.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Boot","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/spring-boot"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Security","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/spring-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Cloud Gateway","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/spring-cloud-gateway"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth 2.0","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"JWT","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/jwt"}],"readingTime":11.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","description":"Add single sign-on to Spring Boot with OpenID Connect and Casdoor: oauth2Login web apps, JWT resource servers and Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, with YAML.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Spring Cloud Gateway","OIDC","OAuth 2.0","SSO","JWT"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Um novo Console, um Marketplace e OAuth para Servidores MCP","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-v4"}},"content":"To add single sign-on to Spring Boot, register each app as an application in an OpenID Connect provider such as Casdoor, point Spring Security at the provider\'s issuer URL, and pick the Spring module that matches the app: `oauth2Login()` for a server-rendered web app, `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` for a REST API that receives bearer tokens, and Spring Cloud Gateway with the `TokenRelay` filter when one gateway signs users in for a set of microservices. Users get SSO because every app sends them to the same Casdoor, which remembers the session: the second app redirects, Casdoor already knows the user, and they come straight back without a login form. This post sets up all three shapes against one Casdoor, each with a working `application.yml` and a customized `SecurityFilterChain`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The three shapes at a glance\\n\\n| Shape | Spring dependency | Who holds the login | Casdoor endpoints used | Filter chain |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Web app | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | The app (HTTP session) | discovery, authorize, token, userinfo, JWKS, logout | `SecurityFilterChain` with `oauth2Login()` |\\n| REST API | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` | Nobody, every request carries a bearer token | JWKS (and discovery if you use `issuer-uri`) | `SecurityFilterChain` with `oauth2ResourceServer()` |\\n| Gateway + microservices | `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` + `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | The gateway (WebFlux session) | Same as the web app; services behind it use JWKS | `SecurityWebFilterChain` with `oauth2Login()`, `TokenRelay` on routes |\\n\\nVersion assumptions: Spring Boot 3.5 with Spring Security 6.5, Java 17, and Spring Cloud 2025.0 (Spring Cloud Gateway 4.3). On Spring Boot 4 the two Security starters are named `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-client` and `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-resource-server`. Gateway 4.3 and 5.0 both use the `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.*` property prefix used below; the older `spring.cloud.gateway.*` prefix and the `spring-cloud-starter-gateway` artifact are deprecated since Spring Cloud 2025.0.\\n\\n## What Casdoor gives Spring\\n\\nIn the Casdoor admin console, create an application (or reuse one) and copy its **Client ID** and **Client secret**. Add every callback the Spring apps will use to **Redirect URLs**. Spring Security\'s default is `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`, so with the registration ID `casdoor` and an app on port 8080 the URL is `http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`. If you use OIDC logout, also add the page users land on afterwards, for example `http://localhost:8080/`; Casdoor refuses a `post_logout_redirect_uri` that is not in the list.\\n\\nCasdoor publishes a standard discovery document at `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so Spring only needs the issuer. These are the endpoints it advertises, taken from the Casdoor source:\\n\\n| Metadata field | Casdoor path |\\n|---|---|\\n| `issuer` | The backend origin, e.g. `https://door.example.com` (no trailing slash) |\\n| `authorization_endpoint` | `/login/oauth/authorize` |\\n| `token_endpoint` | `/api/login/oauth/access_token` |\\n| `userinfo_endpoint` | `/api/userinfo` |\\n| `jwks_uri` | `/.well-known/jwks` |\\n| `end_session_endpoint` | `/api/logout` |\\n\\nSupported scopes are `openid`, `email`, `profile`, `address`, `phone` and `offline_access`, plus any custom scopes defined on the application. Tokens are signed with RS256 by default and carry the cert name as `kid`. Each application also has its own discovery document at `/.well-known/<application>/openid-configuration`; see [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) for the details and the full list of [OAuth 2.0 endpoints](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth).\\n\\nTwo things in Casdoor\'s tokens matter for Spring:\\n\\n- **The issuer must match exactly.** Spring compares the `issuer` in the discovery document with the `issuer-uri` you configure, and later with the `iss` claim of every token. Casdoor derives the issuer from the `origin` setting in `app.conf`, or from the Host header when `origin` is empty. If your Spring apps reach Casdoor through an internal hostname (say `http://casdoor:8000` inside Docker) while browsers use a public one, set `origin` to the public URL so every token gets the same `iss`. See [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n- **Roles come in two forms.** `/api/userinfo` returns `roles` as a list of role names when the `profile` scope was granted. The access token in the default `JWT` [token format](/docs/token/overview) contains `roles` as a list of role objects, each with a `name` field. The code below handles both. Casdoor access tokens also carry `scope` as a space-separated string, which Spring turns into `SCOPE_...` authorities by default.\\n\\nIf you want background on the protocol, see [What is OpenID Connect](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-openid-connect) and [ID token vs access token](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/id-token-vs-access-token).\\n\\n## 1. Spring Boot web app with oauth2Login()\\n\\nAdd `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`. Then configure the provider by issuer:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n```\\n\\nAt startup Spring Boot fetches `https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` and fills in the authorization, token, userinfo and JWKS URIs. Spring then validates the ID token\'s signature, issuer, audience and nonce, calls `/api/userinfo` because `profile` and `email` are in the scope, and merges the claims. `preferred_username` is the Casdoor user name; `name` in userinfo is the display name, and the default `sub` is the user\'s UUID.\\n\\nWithout your own configuration, Spring Boot requires login for every URL. The customized `SecurityFilterChain` below opens the home page, maps Casdoor roles to `ROLE_` authorities, and logs out of Casdoor as well as the app:\\n\\n```java title=\\"SecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class SecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http,\\n            ClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) throws Exception {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/public/**\\", \\"/error\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(login -> login\\n                .userInfoEndpoint(userInfo -> userInfo\\n                    .userAuthoritiesMapper(casdoorRoles())))\\n            .logout(logout -> logout\\n                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private GrantedAuthoritiesMapper casdoorRoles() {\\n        return authorities -> {\\n            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>(authorities);\\n            for (GrantedAuthority authority : authorities) {\\n                if (authority instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc && oidc.getUserInfo() != null) {\\n                    List<String> roles = oidc.getUserInfo().getClaimAsStringList(\\"roles\\");\\n                    if (roles != null) {\\n                        roles.forEach(role -> mapped.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + role)));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return mapped;\\n        };\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`hasRole(\\"admin\\")` now matches users who have the Casdoor role `admin`. `OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler` reads `end_session_endpoint` from the discovery document and redirects to Casdoor\'s `/api/logout` with `id_token_hint` and `post_logout_redirect_uri`, so the Casdoor session ends too. Without it, `/logout` only clears the Spring session and the next login is silent.\\n\\nIn a controller, the signed-in user is an `OidcUser`:\\n\\n```java\\n@GetMapping(\\"/me\\")\\nMap<String, Object> me(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user) {\\n    return Map.of(\\"name\\", user.getName(), \\"email\\", user.getEmail(),\\n            \\"authorities\\", user.getAuthorities().toString());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe older step-by-step page for this setup is [Spring Security OAuth](/docs/integration/java/spring-security/spring-security-oauth).\\n\\n## 2. Spring Boot API as an OAuth2 resource server\\n\\nA REST API should not redirect anyone. It accepts `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`, checks the JWT signature against Casdoor\'s JWKS, and checks the claims. Add `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server`:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 9000\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      resourceserver:\\n        jwt:\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          jwk-set-uri: https://door.example.com/.well-known/jwks\\n          audiences: <client-id>\\n```\\n\\nWith only this file, Spring Boot builds a `JwtDecoder` that checks the signature, the expiry, `iss` and `aud`. Setting `jwk-set-uri` next to `issuer-uri` means the keys are fetched directly instead of through discovery, and the issuer is still validated. The audience is the client ID of the Casdoor application that issued the token: if a single-page app or the gateway from section 3 obtains the token, use that application\'s client ID.\\n\\nThere is one Casdoor-specific check worth adding. Casdoor signs ID tokens and access tokens with the same key and gives both the client ID as audience, so the default validators would also accept an ID token sent as a bearer token. Every Casdoor token has a `tokenType` claim (`access-token`, `id-token` or `refresh-token`), which makes the check simple. Defining a `JwtDecoder` bean replaces Boot\'s, so the bean re-adds the issuer and audience checks:\\n\\n```java title=\\"ApiSecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class ApiSecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri}\\") String issuer,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri}\\") String jwkSetUri,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences}\\") String clientId) {\\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri).build();\\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\\n                JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(JwtClaimNames.AUD,\\n                        aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(clientId)),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<String>(\\"tokenType\\", \\"access-token\\"::equals)));\\n        return decoder;\\n    }\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain apiFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) throws Exception {\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/api/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(server -> server\\n                .jwt(jwt -> jwt\\n                    .decoder(jwtDecoder)\\n                    .jwtAuthenticationConverter(casdoorJwtConverter())))\\n            .sessionManagement(session -> session\\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private JwtAuthenticationConverter casdoorJwtConverter() {\\n        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter scopes = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();\\n        JwtAuthenticationConverter converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\\n        converter.setPrincipalClaimName(\\"name\\");\\n        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\\n            Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = new ArrayList<>(scopes.convert(jwt));\\n            if (jwt.getClaims().get(\\"roles\\") instanceof Collection<?> roles) {\\n                for (Object role : roles) {\\n                    Object name = role instanceof Map<?, ?> map ? map.get(\\"name\\") : role;\\n                    if (name instanceof String roleName && !roleName.isEmpty()) {\\n                        authorities.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + roleName));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return authorities;\\n        });\\n        return converter;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe converter keeps the `SCOPE_` authorities, adds `ROLE_` authorities from the `roles` claim whether it holds names or objects, and uses the `name` claim (the Casdoor user name) as the principal. If the application uses the `JWT-Standard` token format, use `preferred_username` as the principal claim instead. Disabling CSRF and sessions is right here because the API never sees a browser cookie; do not copy those two lines into the web app.\\n\\nTo try it without a browser, get a machine token with the client credentials grant and call the API:\\n\\n```shell\\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -u \'<client-id>:<client-secret>\' \\\\\\n  -d grant_type=client_credentials \\\\\\n  https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token | jq -r .access_token)\\n\\ncurl -H \\"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\\" http://localhost:9000/api/hello\\n```\\n\\nA client credentials token represents the application, not a user, so it has no roles. For more on which claims to check and why, see [JWT validation](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/jwt-validation) and [OAuth scopes](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-scopes).\\n\\n## 3. Spring Cloud Gateway with TokenRelay\\n\\nWith microservices, a common layout is to let the gateway handle login and keep the services as resource servers. The browser only ever talks to the gateway and holds a session cookie; the gateway stores the user\'s access token and the `TokenRelay` filter adds it as `Authorization: Bearer ...` to each proxied request. The services from section 2 validate it as usual.\\n\\nAdd `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`, with the Spring Cloud 2025.0 BOM:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  application:\\n    name: gateway\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n  cloud:\\n    gateway:\\n      server:\\n        webflux:\\n          routes:\\n            - id: orders\\n              uri: http://localhost:9000\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/orders/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n            - id: users\\n              uri: http://localhost:9001\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/users/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n```\\n\\n`TokenRelay=` with no argument relays the token of the signed-in user. The `TokenRelay` filter factory is only created when `spring.security.oauth2.client.*` is configured, and the default authorized-client store is in memory, so a gateway with several replicas needs a shared store or sticky sessions. The services behind it set `audiences` to the gateway\'s client ID.\\n\\nSpring Cloud Gateway runs on WebFlux, so its security configuration is a `SecurityWebFilterChain` built from `ServerHttpSecurity`, not the servlet `SecurityFilterChain`:\\n\\n```java title=\\"GatewaySecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebFluxSecurity\\npublic class GatewaySecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityWebFilterChain gatewayFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http,\\n            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges\\n                .pathMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .anyExchange().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\\n            .logout(logout -> logout.logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nKeep authorization decisions in the services, where the roles are in the token; the gateway only makes sure there is a signed-in user. CSRF protection stays on in the gateway because it is cookie-based.\\n\\nThe existing [Spring Cloud Gateway](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud-gateway) and [Spring Cloud](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud) pages show a different design, where a custom `GlobalFilter` checks a Casdoor user stored in the gateway session.\\n\\n## Alternative: the Casdoor Spring Boot starter\\n\\nIf you would rather not write the YAML, [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter) 2.x (Spring Boot 3 and 4, Java 17+) takes `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.organization-name` and `casdoor.application-name`, and sets the `spring.security.oauth2.*` properties above for whichever Security starter is on the classpath. It registers the client as `casdoor`, maps Casdoor roles to `ROLE_` authorities for both OAuth2 login and the servlet resource server, and exposes the [Casdoor Java SDK](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-java-sdk) services as beans. Its properties have the lowest precedence, so you can still override any of them. See [Spring Boot](/docs/integration/java/spring-boot) for the setup.\\n\\n## Common errors\\n\\n- **\\"The Issuer ... provided in the configuration metadata did not match the requested issuer\\"**: the `issuer-uri` differs from what Casdoor reports. Remove any trailing slash, and set `origin` in Casdoor if the hostnames differ.\\n- **`redirect_uri` rejected by Casdoor**: the callback is not in the application\'s Redirect URLs. Check the scheme and port, especially behind a reverse proxy.\\n- **\\"Missing attribute \'preferred_username\'\\"**: the `profile` scope was not requested, or the application\'s token fields leave out `Name`.\\n- **401 with an `invalid_token` error on the API**: the token came from another application (wrong `aud`), it is expired, or it is an ID token.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Casdoor discovery document and endpoints: [`object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go)\\n- Casdoor token claims: [`object/token_jwt.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/token_jwt.go)\\n- [Spring Boot 3.5 reference: Spring Security, OAuth2 client and resource server](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/3.5/reference/web/spring-security.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OIDC client-initiated logout](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/login/logout.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OAuth 2.0 resource server JWT](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: TokenRelay GatewayFilter factory](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/gatewayfilter-factories/tokenrelay-factory.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: starter `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux`](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/starter.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud 2025.0 release notes](https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-release/wiki/Spring-Cloud-2025.0-Release-Notes)\\n- [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter)"},{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4: Um novo Console, um Marketplace e OAuth para Servidores MCP","description":"O Casdoor v4.0 foi lan\xe7ado em 1\xba de setembro. Dois meses e 25 releases depois, estamos na v4.25, ent\xe3o este \xe9 um bom momento para revisar o que mudou. Em resumo: o console administrativo foi reconstru\xeddo, a auto-hospedagem ficou muito menos complicada, agora existe um Marketplace para integra\xe7\xf5es e temas de login, e o Casdoor pode atuar como servidor de autoriza\xe7\xe3o para servidores MCP e agentes de IA.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":5.29,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4: Um novo Console, um Marketplace e OAuth para Servidores MCP","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Executando o Casdoor no Kubernetes: Helm Chart oficial e Configura\xe7\xe3o como C\xf3digo","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"O Casdoor v4.0 foi lan\xe7ado em 1\xba de setembro. Dois meses e 25 releases depois, estamos na v4.25, ent\xe3o este \xe9 um bom momento para revisar o que mudou. Em resumo: o console administrativo foi reconstru\xeddo, a auto-hospedagem ficou muito menos complicada, agora existe um Marketplace para integra\xe7\xf5es e temas de login, e o Casdoor pode atuar como servidor de autoriza\xe7\xe3o para servidores MCP e agentes de IA.\\n\\nSe voc\xea ainda n\xe3o usou: o Casdoor \xe9 uma plataforma open-source de gest\xe3o de identidade e acesso (IAM) escrita em Go. \xc9 um provedor de identidade completo, n\xe3o uma biblioteca ou um proxy de login. Os usu\xe1rios residem no Casdoor, o Casdoor emite os tokens e seus aplicativos se comunicam com ele via OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP ou SCIM. A autoriza\xe7\xe3o \xe9 tratada pelo [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org), portanto modelos ACL, RBAC e ABAC funcionam. Ele \xe9 licenciado sob Apache-2.0 e est\xe1 listado no CNCF Landscape.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Um console e p\xe1ginas de login reconstru\xeddos {#a-rebuilt-console-and-sign-in-pages}\\n\\nA v4.0 substituiu o console administrativo Ant Design por um novo constru\xeddo com shadcn/ui, Tailwind e Vite (agora com React 19). Possui uma paleta de comandos, sele\xe7\xe3o de linhas em massa, valida\xe7\xe3o de formul\xe1rio inline, formul\xe1rios em duas colunas e tabelas onde voc\xea pode ocultar colunas. Alternar idiomas n\xe3o recarrega mais a p\xe1gina.\\n\\nAs p\xe1ginas de login, cadastro e redefini\xe7\xe3o de senha tamb\xe9m foram redesenhadas. O modo escuro funciona corretamente e as p\xe1ginas s\xe3o utiliz\xe1veis em um celular.\\n\\nSe voc\xea estiver atualizando da v3, leia primeiro o guia de atualiza\xe7\xe3o vinculado ao README. O principal ponto de verifica\xe7\xe3o \xe9 o caminho da compila\xe7\xe3o do frontend se voc\xea servir arquivos est\xe1ticos atrav\xe9s de um CDN ou proxy reverso.\\n\\n## Mais f\xe1cil de hospedar {#easier-to-self-host}\\n\\nMuito do trabalho da v4 veio de issues e threads do f\xf3rum de pessoas rodando o Casdoor elas mesmas:\\n\\n- **Sem senha padr\xe3o.** Na primeira visita, o Casdoor pede que voc\xea defina a senha `admin`, ou voc\xea pode defini-la com `initAdminPassword`.\\n- **Implanta\xe7\xe3o em sub-caminho.** Defina `basePath` para servir o Casdoor em `https://example.com/auth/` em vez de seu pr\xf3prio host.\\n- **Forward auth.** O Casdoor tem um endpoint de forward auth para Traefik, Caddy e Nginx, permitindo colocar atr\xe1s dele aplicativos sem login pr\xf3prio, com caminhos p\xfablicos e regras por site.\\n- **Tags Docker.** As imagens agora tamb\xe9m s\xe3o taggeadas como `X`, `X.Y` e `X.Y.Z`, permitindo seguir uma vers\xe3o major ou minor.\\n- **Helm.** O chart foi movido para o reposit\xf3rio principal, \xe9 publicado no GHCR e no [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) a cada release, e pode manter organiza\xe7\xf5es, aplica\xe7\xf5es e usu\xe1rios como configura\xe7\xe3o. Veja [Executando o Casdoor no Kubernetes](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Servi\xe7o Windows.** O bin\xe1rio pode ser executado como um servi\xe7o do Windows.\\n- **Atualiza\xe7\xf5es.** A p\xe1gina de informa\xe7\xf5es do sistema mostra a \xfaltima release e pode atualizar um bin\xe1rio oficial com um clique.\\n- **Opera\xe7\xf5es.** Verifica\xe7\xf5es de sa\xfade do container, contadores Prometheus para sucesso e falha de login, logs de auditoria encaminhados para Syslog e suporte a Redis Cluster.\\n\\n## Marketplace\\n\\nO novo [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) permite que um administrador instale integra\xe7\xf5es de dentro do Casdoor: templates de aplica\xe7\xe3o, provedores de identidade e temas de login. Instalar um preenche a configura\xe7\xe3o para voc\xea, e desinstalar restaura o que estava l\xe1 antes.\\n\\nExistem mais de 20 temas de login at\xe9 agora. Voc\xea pode visualizar um tema na sua pr\xf3pria p\xe1gina de login antes de instal\xe1-lo. Administradores de organiza\xe7\xe3o tamb\xe9m podem personalizar as p\xe1ginas de login com suas pr\xf3prias fontes, imagens e HTML, dentro de uma allowlist que bloqueia formul\xe1rios e scripts. A demonstra\xe7\xe3o somente leitura em [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) alterna entre os temas se voc\xea quiser v\xea-los.\\n\\n## OAuth para servidores MCP e agentes de IA {#oauth-for-mcp-servers-and-ai-agents}\\n\\nA especifica\xe7\xe3o MCP solicita que servidores MCP remotos usem OAuth, e os clientes MCP geralmente n\xe3o sabem antecipadamente qual servidor de autoriza\xe7\xe3o encontrar\xe3o. O Casdoor agora cobre as pe\xe7as necess\xe1rias para isso:\\n\\n- Fluxo de c\xf3digo de autoriza\xe7\xe3o OAuth 2.1 com PKCE e uma p\xe1gina de consentimento onde os usu\xe1rios podem conceder apenas algumas das escopos solicitadas\\n- Registro Din\xe2mico de Clientes (RFC 7591) e gerenciamento de clientes (RFC 7592)\\n- Documentos de Metadados de ID de Cliente: um cliente MCP pode usar uma URL como seu `client_id` sem registrar-se primeiro\\n- Autentica\xe7\xe3o de cliente `private_key_jwt` para clientes registrados dinamicamente\\n- Indicadores de Recurso (RFC 8707) e Metadados de Recursos Protegidos (RFC 9728)\\n- Troca de Token (RFC 8693), incluindo ID-JAGs para autoriza\xe7\xe3o gerenciada empresarial, para que o IdP de uma organiza\xe7\xe3o possa decidir quais agentes podem acessar quais servidores MCP\\n\\nAponte os metadados de recursos protegidos do seu servidor MCP para o Casdoor, e login, consentimento, emiss\xe3o e valida\xe7\xe3o de tokens s\xe3o tratados para voc\xea. O Casdoor tamb\xe9m executa seu pr\xf3prio servidor MCP, permitindo que assistentes como Claude Code ou Cursor gerenciem usu\xe1rios, aplica\xe7\xf5es e permiss\xf5es atrav\xe9s dele.\\n\\n## Outros trabalhos de protocolo {#other-protocol-work}\\n\\n- Logout \xdanico SAML e SSO iniciado pelo IdP\\n- `prompt=none` OIDC, melhor conformidade para fluxo h\xedbrido, `prompt` e `max_age`, e o par\xe2metro `iss` da RFC 9207\\n- Revoga\xe7\xe3o de token (RFC 7009), DPoP e o fluxo de autoriza\xe7\xe3o de dispositivo\\n- Login por link m\xe1gico, verifica\xe7\xf5es de hist\xf3rico de senhas e passkeys compartilhados entre subdom\xednios irm\xe3os\\n- Login exclusivo com um n\xfamero m\xe1ximo de sess\xf5es por usu\xe1rio\\n\\n## Experimente {#try-it}\\n\\nA maneira mais r\xe1pida de dar uma olhada:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\nEm seguida, abra http://localhost:8000. A imagem inclui SQLite e dados de exemplo, ent\xe3o use-a para experimentar, n\xe3o para produ\xe7\xe3o. O [guia de instala\xe7\xe3o](/docs/basic/server-installation) cobre implanta\xe7\xf5es reais.\\n\\nExistem duas demonstra\xe7\xf5es online: [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) \xe9 edit\xe1vel e reinicia a cada cinco minutos, e [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) \xe9 somente leitura.\\n\\nSe voc\xea preferir n\xe3o executar por conta pr\xf3pria, o [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) oferece uma inst\xe2ncia dedicada e gerenciada a um pre\xe7o fixo, sem taxas por usu\xe1rio. Novas contas recebem $10 em cr\xe9dito para testar.\\n\\n## Liga\xe7\xf5es {#links}\\n\\n- GitHub: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Notas de release: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs: https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord: https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nRelat\xf3rios de bugs e solicita\xe7\xf5es de recursos s\xe3o bem-vindos no GitHub. Se o Casdoor for \xfatil para voc\xea, uma estrela ajuda outras pessoas a encontr\xe1-lo."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Executando o Casdoor no Kubernetes: Helm Chart oficial e Configura\xe7\xe3o como C\xf3digo","description":"O Casdoor possui um Helm chart oficial. Ele reside no reposit\xf3rio principal em manifests/casdoor, \xe9 publicado a cada lan\xe7amento do Casdoor no GHCR e est\xe1 listado no Artifact Hub. Come\xe7ando com o Casdoor v4.18.0, o chart tamb\xe9m pode manter suas organiza\xe7\xf5es, aplica\xe7\xf5es, usu\xe1rios e provedores como configura\xe7\xe3o declarativa: altere-os no arquivo values, execute helm upgrade, e os pods em execu\xe7\xe3o aplicam a mudan\xe7a sem reiniciar.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Executando o Casdoor no Kubernetes: Helm Chart oficial e Configura\xe7\xe3o como C\xf3digo","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Um novo Console, um Marketplace e OAuth para Servidores MCP","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor entra no CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"O Casdoor possui um Helm chart oficial. Ele reside no reposit\xf3rio principal em [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), \xe9 publicado a cada lan\xe7amento do Casdoor no GHCR e est\xe1 listado no [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). Come\xe7ando com o Casdoor v4.18.0, o chart tamb\xe9m pode manter suas organiza\xe7\xf5es, aplica\xe7\xf5es, usu\xe1rios e provedores como configura\xe7\xe3o declarativa: altere-os no arquivo values, execute `helm upgrade`, e os pods em execu\xe7\xe3o aplicam a mudan\xe7a sem reiniciar.\\n\\nEste post percorre uma implanta\xe7\xe3o no Kubernetes desde o primeiro `helm install` at\xe9 uma configura\xe7\xe3o que voc\xea pode manter no Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Instalar {#install}\\n\\nVoc\xea precisa do Helm v3.8+ e de um cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nA vers\xe3o do chart \xe9 a vers\xe3o do Casdoor, ent\xe3o `--version 4.18.0` instala o Casdoor 4.18.0. Sem um Ingress ou Gateway, o servi\xe7o s\xf3 \xe9 acess\xedvel dentro do cluster. Para test\xe1-lo, encaminhe uma porta:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nDepois abra `http://localhost:8000`. Na primeira visita, o Casdoor pede para definir a senha de `built-in/admin`; ent\xe3o fa\xe7a login com ela. Veja [Entrar no console administrativo](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## Uma configura\xe7\xe3o de produ\xe7\xe3o {#a-production-setup}\\n\\nOs padr\xf5es s\xe3o destinados a um teste r\xe1pido: o Casdoor armazena seus dados em SQLite dentro do cont\xeainer, portanto eles s\xe3o perdidos quando o pod \xe9 substitu\xeddo. Para uma implanta\xe7\xe3o real, coloque as configura\xe7\xf5es abaixo em um arquivo `values.yaml` e instale com `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### Banco de dados externo {#external-database}\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nPara manter a senha do banco de dados fora do arquivo values, coloque todo o `app.conf` em um Secret e defina `configFromSecret` com o nome dele.\\n\\n### Expondo o Casdoor {#exposing-casdoor}\\n\\nO chart suporta tanto um Ingress cl\xe1ssico quanto a API Gateway. Com um controlador Ingress e cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nCom a API Gateway (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric e outros), anexe um `HTTPRoute` a um Gateway existente:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nO chart tamb\xe9m pode criar o Gateway ele mesmo e adicionar um redirecionamento de HTTP para HTTPS, veja o [guia do Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### Mais de uma r\xe9plica {#more-than-one-replica}\\n\\nPor padr\xe3o, o Casdoor mant\xe9m sess\xf5es de login no disco local. Antes de executar mais de uma r\xe9plica, aponte todas elas para o mesmo Redis, para que um usu\xe1rio permane\xe7a autenticado independentemente do pod que atender \xe0 solicita\xe7\xe3o. Cada configura\xe7\xe3o do `app.conf` pode ser passada como uma vari\xe1vel de ambiente, ent\xe3o o endpoint pode vir de um Secret:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nO mesmo Redis \xe9 usado para o estado do fluxo de dispositivo OAuth. Uma vez que o Redis esteja configurado, `autoscaling.enabled: true` adiciona um HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configura\xe7\xe3o como c\xf3digo {#configuration-as-code}\\n\\nClicar em organiza\xe7\xf5es e aplica\xe7\xf5es juntos na interface web \xe9 bom para um primeiro teste, mas a maioria das equipes deseja que a configura\xe7\xe3o de seu provedor de identidade seja revisada e versionada como o resto de sua infraestrutura. Os valores `initData` fazem isso:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nO chart armazena os dados em um Secret e monta-o nos pods. O Casdoor aplica-os na inicializa\xe7\xe3o, depois verifica-os a cada 30 segundos (`initData.watchInterval`). Quando voc\xea altera os dados e executa `helm upgrade`, o kubelet atualiza o Secret montado e os pods em execu\xe7\xe3o aplicam-no sem reiniciar.\\n\\nAlgumas regras tornam isso seguro de usar junto com a interface web:\\n\\n- **Somente os campos que voc\xea escreve s\xe3o gerenciados.** Um objeto que j\xe1 existe recebe os campos definidos no arquivo; seus outros campos mant\xeam os valores editados na interface. Voc\xea pode gerenciar apenas os URIs de redirecionamento de uma aplica\xe7\xe3o e deixar o resto para o console administrativo.\\n- **As senhas s\xe3o senhas iniciais.** A `password` de um usu\xe1rio \xe9 usada apenas quando o usu\xe1rio \xe9 criado, para que os usu\xe1rios possam alter\xe1-la posteriormente.\\n- **Nada \xe9 exclu\xeddo.** Remover um objeto do arquivo n\xe3o o remove do Casdoor.\\n- **Erros n\xe3o derrubam o Casdoor.** Se um objeto falhar ao ser aplicado, o erro \xe9 registrado e o Casdoor continua atendendo; os dados s\xe3o tentados novamente a cada verifica\xe7\xe3o at\xe9 serem aplicados.\\n\\nO formato do arquivo \xe9 aquele que o Casdoor sempre usou para [inicializa\xe7\xe3o de dados](/docs/deployment/data-initialization): organiza\xe7\xf5es, aplica\xe7\xf5es, usu\xe1rios, provedores, certificados, pap\xe9is, permiss\xf5es, grupos, servidores LDAP, webhooks, planos, pre\xe7os e mais, com os mesmos nomes de campos da API REST. Uma maneira r\xe1pida de come\xe7ar \xe9 exportar uma inst\xe2ncia existente com `casdoor -export` e manter os objetos que voc\xea deseja gerenciar.\\n\\nPara manter os dados fora do arquivo de valores, por exemplo, quando gerenciado pelo Sealed Secrets ou External Secrets, crie o Secret voc\xea mesmo:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nO mesmo recurso funciona sem Kubernetes. Monte um arquivo JSON ou YAML e defina:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Ou use Terraform {#or-use-terraform}\\n\\nSe voc\xea prefere `terraform plan`, importa\xe7\xf5es de objetos existentes e detec\xe7\xe3o de drift, o [provedor oficial do Terraform](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) gerencia os mesmos objetos atrav\xe9s da API do Casdoor:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse os valores do Helm quando a configura\xe7\xe3o deve acompanhar a implanta\xe7\xe3o, e Terraform quando o Casdoor \xe9 um dos muitos sistemas que voc\xea j\xe1 gerencia com ele. Veja o [guia do Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Vindo do chart antigo {#coming-from-the-old-chart}\\n\\nAt\xe9 a v4.15.0, o chart era publicado como `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, e o reposit\xf3rio separado `casdoor/casdoor-helm` agora est\xe1 arquivado. O chart no Docker Hub ainda recebe todas as vers\xf5es, mas novas instala\xe7\xf5es devem usar o do GHCR. Migrar uma vers\xe3o existente \xe9 uma atualiza\xe7\xe3o regular, e os nomes dos recursos permanecem os mesmos:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Liga\xe7\xf5es {#links}\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [c\xf3digo-fonte](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guias: [Experimente com Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Inicializa\xe7\xe3o de dados e configura\xe7\xe3o como c\xf3digo](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- N\xe3o quer executar voc\xea mesmo? O [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) oferece uma inst\xe2ncia dedicada do Casdoor que n\xf3s hospedamos e mantemos atualizada para voc\xea."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor entra no CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"Em 22 de fevereiro de 2026, o Casdoor entrou no CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, na categoria Security & Compliance dentro de Provisioning. O Landscape re\xfane projetos e produtos cloud-native amplamente utilizados; a inclus\xe3o ajuda usu\xe1rios e empresas a descobrirem e avaliarem solu\xe7\xf5es para construir sistemas cloud-native.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":2.1,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor entra no CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Executando o Casdoor no Kubernetes: Helm Chart oficial e Configura\xe7\xe3o como C\xf3digo","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Melhorando a Seguran\xe7a do Aplicativo e a Experi\xeancia do Usu\xe1rio com o SSO de Autentica\xe7\xe3o Facial do Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Em 22 de fevereiro de 2026, o **Casdoor** entrou no [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), na categoria **Security & Compliance** dentro de Provisioning. O Landscape re\xfane projetos e produtos cloud-native amplamente utilizados; a inclus\xe3o ajuda usu\xe1rios e empresas a descobrirem e avaliarem solu\xe7\xf5es para construir sistemas cloud-native.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## Sobre o Casdoor {#about-casdoor}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor \xe9 uma plataforma open-source de Gest\xe3o de Identidade e Acesso (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO) focada em interface gr\xe1fica, com um web UI. Suporta OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML e CAS, al\xe9m de muitos logins sociais (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), e ajuda voc\xea a construir gest\xe3o de usu\xe1rios e SSO em poucos passos. Casdoor \xe9 desenvolvido pelo [Casbin](https://casbin.org) em Golang e React. Usa uma arquitetura de separa\xe7\xe3o front-end/back-end, suporta alta concorr\xeancia e \xe9 mantido ativamente pela comunidade. O projeto recebeu mais de 13k estrelas no GitHub.\\n\\n## Sobre a CNCF e o Cloud Native Landscape {#about-cncf-and-the-cloud-native-landscape}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nA **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** \xe9 um projeto da Linux Foundation que promove tecnologias cloud-native. Sua vis\xe3o \xe9 tornar o cloud native onipresente. A CNCF mant\xe9m o **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), que classifica e rastreia projetos e produtos no ecossistema cloud-native e serve como um mapa de refer\xeancia para desenvolvedores e empresas.\\n\\n## Olhando para o futuro {#looking-ahead}\\n\\nEntrar no CNCF Landscape reflete o progresso da maturidade e da comunidade do Casdoor. Continuaremos melhorando o Casdoor e sua integra\xe7\xe3o com fluxos de trabalho cloud-native, e damos as boas-vindas a mais usu\xe1rios e contribuidores para se juntarem \xe0 comunidade.\\n\\nLinks relacionados:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documenta\xe7\xe3o](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Melhorando a Seguran\xe7a do Aplicativo e a Experi\xeancia do Usu\xe1rio com o SSO de Autentica\xe7\xe3o Facial do Casdoor","description":"Em uma era onde a gest\xe3o de identidade digital \xe9 crucial, solu\xe7\xf5es de Single Sign-On (SSO) como o Casdoor est\xe3o revolucionando a maneira como os usu\xe1rios interagem com m\xfaltiplos aplicativos. O SSO do Casdoor simplifica o processo de login permitindo que os usu\xe1rios se autentiquem apenas uma vez para acessar m\xfaltiplos servi\xe7os. Isso n\xe3o apenas aumenta a conveni\xeancia do usu\xe1rio, mas tamb\xe9m impulsiona o engajamento e a satisfa\xe7\xe3o.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":1.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Melhorando a Seguran\xe7a do Aplicativo e a Experi\xeancia do Usu\xe1rio com o SSO de Autentica\xe7\xe3o Facial do Casdoor","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor entra no CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/pt/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bem-vindo","permalink":"/pt/blog/welcome"}},"content":"Em uma era onde a gest\xe3o de identidade digital \xe9 crucial, solu\xe7\xf5es de Single Sign-On (SSO) como o Casdoor est\xe3o revolucionando a maneira como os usu\xe1rios interagem com m\xfaltiplos aplicativos. O SSO do Casdoor simplifica o processo de login permitindo que os usu\xe1rios se autentiquem apenas uma vez para acessar m\xfaltiplos servi\xe7os. Isso n\xe3o apenas aumenta a conveni\xeancia do usu\xe1rio, mas tamb\xe9m impulsiona o engajamento e a satisfa\xe7\xe3o.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nPara as empresas, o SSO do Casdoor racionaliza a gest\xe3o de credenciais dos usu\xe1rios, melhorando a seguran\xe7a e simplificando a integra\xe7\xe3o de novos usu\xe1rios. Ele contraria o risco associado a senhas fracas ou recicladas centralizando a gest\xe3o de usu\xe1rios.\\n\\nNo entanto, diante das crescentes amea\xe7as online, integrar medidas de seguran\xe7a robustas em sistemas SSO como o Casdoor \xe9 vital. \xc9 aqui que a tecnologia de autentica\xe7\xe3o facial entra em jogo, adicionando uma camada extra de prote\xe7\xe3o enquanto mant\xe9m intera\xe7\xf5es amig\xe1veis com o usu\xe1rio.\\n\\nA autentica\xe7\xe3o facial emprega caracter\xedsticas faciais \xfanicas para confirmar as identidades dos usu\xe1rios. Ela compara imagens ao vivo com modelos faciais armazenados, oferecendo uma maneira segura e simples de autenticar usu\xe1rios. Este m\xe9todo possui alta precis\xe3o, mesmo sob condi\xe7\xf5es variadas, e \xe9 amplamente adotado em dispositivos modernos do consumidor.\\n\\nIntegrar a autentica\xe7\xe3o facial no sistema SSO do Casdoor promete uma experi\xeancia de usu\xe1rio segura, eficiente e cont\xednua. Para garantir um desempenho \xf3timo, \xe9 crucial focar em:\\n\\n1. _Medidas Anti-Spoofing_: Implementar detec\xe7\xe3o de vivacidade, como rastreamento de movimentos dos olhos ou gestos aleat\xf3rios, \xe9 essencial para contrariar tentativas de spoofing e manter a integridade do sistema.\\n\\n2. _Atualiza\xe7\xf5es Regulares para Melhorar a Precis\xe3o_: Manter a tecnologia de autentica\xe7\xe3o facial atualizada com os \xfaltimos avan\xe7os em IA e biometria garante precis\xe3o e melhora a experi\xeancia do usu\xe1rio. \xc9 vital usar conjuntos de dados diversos para o treinamento de algoritmos para prevenir vieses.\\n\\n3. _Equilibrando Seguran\xe7a e Conveni\xeancia_: Os melhores sistemas encontram um equil\xedbrio perfeito entre medidas de seguran\xe7a rigorosas e a conveni\xeancia do usu\xe1rio. Essa abordagem incentiva a ado\xe7\xe3o do usu\xe1rio garantindo um processo de autentica\xe7\xe3o direto sem comprometer a seguran\xe7a.\\n\\nEm conclus\xe3o, integrando a autentica\xe7\xe3o facial com o SSO do Casdoor, as empresas podem oferecer uma experi\xeancia de login segura, eficiente e amig\xe1vel ao usu\xe1rio. Isso n\xe3o apenas fortalece a seguran\xe7a, mas tamb\xe9m est\xe1 alinhado com as expectativas modernas dos usu\xe1rios no mundo digital."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/pt/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/pt/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Bem-vindo","description":"Bem-vindo ao site oficial do Casdoor!\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/pt/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.59,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Bem-vindo","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Melhorando a Seguran\xe7a do Aplicativo e a Experi\xeancia do Usu\xe1rio com o SSO de Autentica\xe7\xe3o Facial do Casdoor","permalink":"/pt/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Bem-vindo ao site oficial do Casdoor!\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## O que \xe9 o Casdoor? {#what-is-casdoor}\\n\\nCasdoor \xe9 uma plataforma de [Gest\xe3o de Identidade e Acesso (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) focada em interface gr\xe1fica, baseada em OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML e CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor \xe9 desenvolvido pelo [Casbin](https://casbin.org) usando Golang e React.\\n\\nO Casdoor visa fornecer uma plataforma de autentica\xe7\xe3o poderosa para lidar com o gerenciamento de usu\xe1rios.\\n\\n## Por que usar o Casdoor? {#why-use-casdoor}\\n\\n* \u26A1\uFE0F Recurso poderoso e configura\xe7\xe3o simples. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Arquitetura de separa\xe7\xe3o front-end back-end. O Casdoor fornece uma UI manobr\xe1vel e suporta alta concorr\xeancia.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Comunidade open source ativa. O Casdoor \xe9 open source e mantido ativamente.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Suporte a m\xfaltiplos idiomas. O Casdoor suporta v\xe1rios idiomas, incluindo chin\xeas, ingl\xeas, franc\xeas e muitos outros.\\n\\n\\n## Como usar o Casdoor? {#how-to-use-casdoor}\\n\\nVoc\xea pode seguir a documenta\xe7\xe3o detalhada para conhecer a [API do Casdoor](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) e [configurar seu pr\xf3prio Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). Ou utilize os [SDKs do Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) para implementar sua pr\xf3pria aplica\xe7\xe3o."}]}}')}}]);